  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The threat for severe weather will diminish and \nend as we progress through the evening. \n\nThe threat for severe storms is waning as there is a lack of \ninstability. However...organized storms will still be possible due \nto decent shear values 45-50 knots. The main batch of storms will \nmove off to our east this evening and we may see a few more storms \nwith the weak cold front. After that...it looks like we will see \ndiminishing storm coverage east of the lake and relatively quiet \nweather overnight. Although, lingering moisture behind the front \nwill produce low cloudiness and maybe some pockets of drizzle. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Colder weather by the end of the week with some \npotential for snow. \n\nA deepening upper level trough will progress eastward into the Great \nLakes region Thursday night and Friday. The associated surface low \nwill strengthen across the Great Lakes, supporting a sharp cold \nfront to sweep through on Friday. Confidence remains low at this \ntime as model discrepancies with the timing, strength and placement \nof this system persists. Regardless, the atmospheric pattern will \nlikely bring the potential for strong winds Friday into Saturday. \nAdditionally, a much colder airmass will surge into the area in the \nwake of the frontal passage with pre-frontal synoptic rain showers \neventually giving way to lake effect rain and snow showers.",
